    {
      "id": "063f62fb81179bdb96654ad9f0baab02faa0a5bc",
      "individual_note": true,
      "resolvable": false,
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 862225878,
          "type": null,
          "body": "https://gitlab.com/wireshark/wireshark/-/blob/master/ui/qt/preferences_dialog.ui#L19  \n`     <widget class=\"PrefModuleTreeView\" name=\"prefsView\">`  \n  \nhttps://gitlab.com/wireshark/wireshark/-/blob/master/ui/qt/widgets/pref_module_view.h#L18  \n`class PrefModuleTreeView : public QTreeView`  \n  \nhttps://doc.qt.io/qt-5/qtreeview.html#keyboardSearch  \n\"Reimplements: QAbstractItemView::keyboardSearch(const QString &search).\"  \n  \nhttps://doc.qt.io/qt-5/qabstractitemview.html#keyboardSearch  \n\"In the default implementation, the search is reset if search is empty, or the time interval since the last search has exceeded QApplication::keyboardInputInterval().\"  \n  \nhttps://doc.qt.io/qt-5/qapplication.html#keyboardInputInterval-prop  \n\"The default value on X11 is 400 milliseconds. On Windows and Mac OS, the operating system's value is used.\"  \n  \nSo it works out of the box on Ubuntu. Also works with code below on Windows:  \n```\n#if defined(Q_OS_WIN)\n    QApplication::setKeyboardInputInterval(400);\n#endif\n```\n  \nBut what are \"On Windows and Mac OS, the operating system's value is used.\" ???  \nIf they can be tracked down, then it's a documentation update telling user where to tweak the timer.  \nOr just hard code the input interval (or add a Gui preference?) in the code.",
          "author": {
            "id": 6701257,
            "username": "chuckcraft",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Chuck Craft",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/0809ffc6d9d98013a1895132bc055c622830b8a20bfa49328f53d5eb4a1206cb?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/chuckcraft"
          },
